# GSAP Expert — Aurora Animation Standard v2.0

This skill is the single source of truth for all animation work within the Aurora ecosystem. Derived from the official `greensock/gsap-skills` repository, calibrated with Emil Kowalski's design engineering principles, and hardened for the Dark Editorial DNA.

## 1. Mission

**"Does this animation serve the user's comprehension — or just the developer's ego?"**

Every animation must pass this gate before a single line of GSAP is written.

## 2. Banned Patterns

- DO NOT animate `top`, `left`, `width`, `height`, `margin`, or `padding` — use `transform` and `opacity` exclusively.
- DO NOT use `ease-in` for UI element entry — it starts slow, feels sluggish. Use `ease-out` or custom curves.
- DO NOT use `transition: all` — specify exact properties: `transition: transform 200ms ease-out`.
- DO NOT animate from `scale(0)` — start from `scale(0.95)` with `opacity: 0`. Nothing appears from nothing.
- DO NOT use `ease: "bounce"` or `ease: "elastic"` without explicit narrative justification — they communicate playfulness, not authority.
- DO NOT animate keyboard-initiated actions (command palette, shortcuts) — these happen 100+ times/day. Zero animation.
- DO NOT use duration > 300ms for standard UI interactions — the interface must feel instant.
- DO NOT create tweens inside scroll handlers — use `gsap.quickTo()` for high-frequency updates.
- DO NOT use `linear` easing for UI elements — it feels mechanical and artificial.
- DO NOT skip `prefers-reduced-motion` — accessibility is not optional.

## 3. Animation Decision Framework

Before writing any GSAP code, answer these questions in order:

### 3.1 Should this animate at all?

| Frequency of Use | Decision |
|---|---|
| 100+ times/day (keyboard shortcuts, toggles) | **No animation. Ever.** |
| Tens of times/day (hover, list navigation) | Remove or reduce to < 100ms |
| Occasional (modals, drawers, toasts) | Standard animation (150-300ms) |
| Rare / first-time (onboarding, celebrations) | Can add delight (300-500ms) |

### 3.2 What easing should it use?

| Context | Easing | GSAP Value |
|---|---|---|
| Element **entering** viewport | ease-out | `power2.out` or custom |
| Element **exiting** viewport | ease-in (fast start) | `power2.in` |
| Element **moving/morphing** on screen | ease-in-out | `power2.inOut` |
| Hover / color change | ease | `power1.out` |
| Constant motion (marquee, progress) | linear | `none` |
| **Default fallback** | ease-out | `power2.out` |

### 3.3 Custom Easing Curves (Mandatory)

Built-in CSS easings are too weak. Use these calibrated curves:

```css
/* Strong ease-out — UI interactions, element entry */
--ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1);

/* Strong ease-in-out — on-screen movement, morphing */
--ease-in-out: cubic-bezier(0.77, 0, 0.175, 1);

/* iOS-like drawer — panels, sheets, drawers */
--ease-drawer: cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1);

/* Aurora Sovereign — Dark Editorial signature */
--ease-sovereign: cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94);

/* Reveal — scroll-triggered content entry */
--ease-reveal: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
```

GSAP equivalent:
```javascript
gsap.registerEffect({
  name: "revealUp",
  effect: (targets, config) => {
    return gsap.fromTo(targets, 
      { y: config.y, opacity: 0 },
      { 
        y: 0, opacity: 1, 
        duration: config.duration,
        ease: "power2.out",
        stagger: config.stagger 
      }
    );
  },
  defaults: { y: 24, duration: 0.8, stagger: 0.08 }
});
```

## 4. Duration Lookup Table

| Element Type | Duration | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Button press feedback (`:active`) | 100–160ms | Tactile, instant |
| Tooltips, small popovers | 125–200ms | Quick reveal |
| Dropdowns, selects | 150–250ms | Responsive |
| Modals, drawers | 200–400ms | Can breathe |
| Scroll reveal (section entry) | 600–1000ms | Deliberate |
| Page transitions | 400–800ms | Cinematic |
| Preloader sequences | 3000–8000ms | Narrative |
| Marketing/explanatory | Open | Match the story |

**Rule: UI animations MUST stay under 300ms.** A 180ms dropdown feels more responsive than a 400ms one.

## 5. Aurora-Specific: Rule of Silence (0.8s)

The Dark Editorial DNA mandates a **minimum 0.8s of absolute visual rest** before any substantial motion or reveal. This is the silence that gives weight to the movement.

```javascript
// Hero entrance — Rule of Silence enforced
const heroTimeline = gsap.timeline({ delay: 0.8 });

heroTimeline
  .fromTo(".hero-headline", 
    { y: 40, opacity: 0 },
    { y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 1.0, ease: "power2.out" }
  )
  .fromTo(".hero-subline",
    { y: 24, opacity: 0 },
    { y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power2.out" },
    "-=0.4" // overlap — stagger, not sequential
  );
```

## 6. React & Astro Integration

### 6.1 useGSAP Hook (Mandatory)

```javascript
import { useGSAP } from "@gsap/react";
import { useRef } from "react";

function HeroSection() {
  const containerRef = useRef(null);

  useGSAP((context, contextSafe) => {
    // All selectors scoped to containerRef — no leakage
    gsap.fromTo(".headline", 
      { y: 40, opacity: 0 },
      { y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 1.0, ease: "power2.out", delay: 0.8 }
    );

    // Event handlers MUST use contextSafe
    const onHover = contextSafe(() => {
      gsap.to(".cta-button", { scale: 1.02, duration: 0.2 });
    });
  }, { scope: containerRef });

  return <section ref={containerRef}>...</section>;
}
```

### 6.2 Cleanup Contract

- Always use `gsap.context()` for automatic cleanup.
- Never create tweens outside `useGSAP` or `useEffect` with context.
- Kill ScrollTriggers explicitly when component unmounts.

## 7. ScrollTrigger Patterns

### 7.1 Batch Reveal (Lists and Grids)

```javascript
ScrollTrigger.batch("[data-reveal]", {
  onEnter: (batch) => {
    gsap.fromTo(batch,
      { y: 24, opacity: 0 },
      { y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power2.out", stagger: 0.08 }
    );
  },
  start: "top 85%",
  once: true
});
```

### 7.2 Pinning (Section Lock)

```javascript
ScrollTrigger.create({
  trigger: ".pinned-section",
  pin: true,
  start: "top top",
  end: "+=200%",
  scrub: 1  // physical, weighted scroll — never scrub: true (too snappy)
});
```

### 7.3 Parallax Depth

```javascript
gsap.to(".parallax-bg", {
  y: "-30%",
  ease: "none",
  scrollTrigger: {
    trigger: ".parallax-section",
    start: "top bottom",
    end: "bottom top",
    scrub: 1
  }
});
```

### 7.4 Dynamic Content Refresh

```javascript
// ALWAYS refresh after dynamic content loads (images, data)
ScrollTrigger.refresh();

// For async content:
ScrollTrigger.addEventListener("refreshInit", () => {
  // Recalculate positions
});
```

## 8. Stagger Orchestration

Never mount lists instantly. Use cascade delays for waterfall reveals.

```javascript
// Stagger with controlled cascade
gsap.fromTo(".card", 
  { y: 24, opacity: 0 },
  { 
    y: 0, opacity: 1,
    duration: 0.6,
    ease: "power2.out",
    stagger: {
      amount: 0.4,      // total stagger time — NOT per-item
      from: "start",     // or "center", "edges", "random"
      ease: "power1.in"  // stagger easing — items accelerate
    }
  }
);
```

**Stagger constraints:**
- Delay between items: 30–80ms (shorter = snappier)
- Total stagger for a group of 6+ items: cap at 400ms
- Never block interaction while stagger plays

## 9. Performance Rules

### 9.1 GPU-Only Properties

Animate exclusively:
- `transform` (x, y, scale, rotation, skew)
- `opacity`
- `clip-path` (hardware-accelerated in modern browsers)

### 9.2 High-Frequency Motion

```javascript
// For mouse followers, scroll-linked transforms:
const xTo = gsap.quickTo(".follower", "x", { duration: 0.4, ease: "power2.out" });
const yTo = gsap.quickTo(".follower", "y", { duration: 0.4, ease: "power2.out" });

window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
  xTo(e.clientX);
  yTo(e.clientY);
});
```

### 9.3 will-change Management

```css
/* Apply ONLY on actively animating elements */
.animating { will-change: transform, opacity; }

/* Remove after animation completes */
.done { will-change: auto; }
```

### 9.4 CSS vs JS Decision

| Scenario | Use |
|---|---|
| Predetermined, one-shot animation | CSS `@keyframes` (off main thread) |
| Dynamic, interruptible animation | GSAP (main thread, but controllable) |
| Rapidly triggered (toasts, toggles) | CSS transitions (interruptible, no restart) |
| Scroll-linked | GSAP ScrollTrigger (precise control) |
| Mouse-tracking, drag | GSAP `quickTo` + spring physics |

## 10. Accessibility

```javascript
// Check BEFORE creating animations
const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

if (prefersReduced) {
  // Instant state — no motion, keep opacity transitions only
  gsap.set("[data-reveal]", { opacity: 1, y: 0 });
} else {
  // Full animation pipeline
  initRevealAnimations();
}
```

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}
```

## 11. Review Checklist

When reviewing animation code, verify:

| Issue | Fix | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| `transition: all` or no specific property | Specify exact properties | High |
| `scale(0)` entry | Start from `scale(0.95)` + `opacity: 0` | High |
| `ease-in` on entering element | Switch to `ease-out` or custom curve | High |
| Duration > 300ms on UI element | Reduce to 150–250ms | High |
| No `prefers-reduced-motion` handler | Add media query + JS check | Critical |
| Animation on keyboard action | Remove animation entirely | High |
| Tween created in scroll handler | Use `gsap.quickTo()` | Medium |
| Missing `scope` in `useGSAP` | Add `{ scope: containerRef }` | Medium |
| Keyframes on rapidly-triggered element | Use CSS transitions | Medium |
| Missing Rule of Silence (0.8s delay) | Add `delay: 0.8` to hero timeline | Medium |
| `will-change` left on inactive elements | Remove after animation | Low |
| Stagger > 100ms per item | Reduce to 30–80ms | Low |

